(allocated, their length and element references cannot change. Consequently, CPython) Tj
T*
(allocates exact memory blocks for tuples, reducing memory footprint and accelerating) Tj
T*
(creation time. Furthermore, immutable tuples containing only hashable objects are) Tj
T*
(themselves hashable, allowing them to serve as dictionary keys or set elements, whereas) Tj
T*
(lists are unhashable and raise TypeError.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain the internal hash table architecture of Python dictionaries and how) Tj
T*
(hash collisions are resolved.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Python dictionaries use a compact hash table structure. Keys are hashed via) Tj
T*
(hash\(key\) to generate a pseudo-random integer. In modern Python \(3.6+\), dictionaries) Tj
T*
(maintain two arrays: a sparse indices array and a dense entries array storing [hash,) Tj
T*
(key, value] in insertion order, preserving key insertion order while saving ~30-50%) Tj
T*
(memory. When two distinct keys produce the same hash index \(hash collision\), Python) Tj
T*
(resolves it using Open Addressing with a pseudo-random perturb sequence: j = \(\(5*j\) + 1) Tj
T*
(+ perturb\) % table_size. Lookup continues along the probe chain until the key is found) Tj
T*
(or an empty slot is encountered.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
